{
  "rationale": "Evidence from iteration 0 (aggregated, no per-step telemetry): only 2 of 60 trials finished, and 58 ended off track. Progress averaged 0.37 on the oval, 0.165 on the s-bend and 0.12 on the slalom. Max error sat at about 0.06 m on every track, which is the off-track limit for this body (max(0.06, 0.0125 + 0.12/4) = 0.06). Line-loss events averaged about 1 per run, so the robot leaves the tape on bends and does not recover. RMS error stayed low (0.027 to 0.031), so the robot tracks well on straights and fails when the curvature grows. The likely causes are: (1) the five sensors span only ±0.04 m, so on a sharp bend the tape leaves the array before the steering responds; (2) kp = 2.5 with ki = 0 gives too little corrective authority once error builds, and the fixed left/right motor mismatch causes a steady drift that the controller never removes; (3) base_speed 1.3 may be too fast for the sharpest bends with this response.

Changes: (a) widen the array to seven 16 mm sensors spanning ±0.06 m, placed 0.06 m ahead of the axle, so the tape stays under a sensor through tighter bends and the larger footprint gives more non-zero readings when the line is off centre, which should reduce line losses; (b) set sensor weights to a linear ramp from -2 to +2 so the signal scales with lateral offset; (c) raise kp from 2.5 to 3.5 for more correction on bends; (d) add a small ki = 0.1 to cancel the motor mismatch drift; (e) raise kd from 0.2 to 0.5 to damp the faster response; (f) lower base_speed from 1.3 to 1.2 to trade a little speed for tracking; (g) reduce mass to 0.7 kg and body_length to 0.16 m, which lowers yaw inertia so the robot turns more readily. Body width stays at 0.12 m, which keeps the off-track limit at 0.06 m (the maximum body width of 0.18 m would not raise it above that).

Expected result: higher finish rate on the oval, a meaningful increase in s-bend and slalom progress, and fewer line-loss events. Hypotheses to test next: H1, the wider sensor span is the main fix for off-track exits on bends; H2, the ki term removes the drift and reduces RMS error; H3, kd = 0.5 may add jitter, which steering_oscillation will reveal. Next measurements: completion and progress per track to check the finish rate, line_loss_events to check whether the wider array keeps the line, steering_oscillation to test kd, and max_error to see whether the robot still reaches the 0.06 m limit.",
